WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.903
A linear system asks for an input satisfying several constraints at once

00:00:05.903 --> 00:00:08.655
Each equation here describes a line

00:00:08.655 --> 00:00:15.533
Their shared solution is the intersection point, where both equations return the required values simultaneously.

00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:21.938
Adding the equations cancels y and produces two x equals four

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:26.579
Keep one original equation to recover y equal to one

00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:34.933
These elimination steps preserve solutions because the equation replacements are reversible; we are not arbitrarily discarding a constraint.

00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:41.467
The matrix viewpoint asks which vector is transformed into the target vector

00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:47.136
The determinant here is negative two, not zero, so the transformation is invertible

00:00:47.136 --> 00:00:51.933
Every target has one preimage, matching the unique intersection we found.

00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:56.447
Dependent left-hand sides can produce a different outcome

00:00:56.447 --> 00:01:02.472
Doubling the first equation would require the second right-hand side to be six, not eight

00:01:02.472 --> 00:01:09.300
These parallel lines never meet; if it were six instead, the constraints would describe the same line.
